6068938 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "155e5337-d5e8-53d9-b3b1-ee0f86219c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069358 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069358 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069358 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069358 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation ${3}^{x}=\\frac{1}{27}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069358 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7238785-3d78-5ef3-9612-1e7b810378dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069358 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $27$ as ${3}^{3}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {3}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}} \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={3}^{-3} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069424 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c88910e9-93dc-5fa8-9def-79b320b71eb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069424 #edb/ref 1074 "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{4}{b})}^{3}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{4}{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069625 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069625 #edb/ref 1061 170031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069625 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddaa7aab-6a5c-5c05-989d-9d15267eceec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069625 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069626 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069626 #edb/ref 1061 170032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069626 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2856989-c0e4-57bc-927d-5351b29a50a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069626 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069627 #edb/ref 1061 170035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "792c4b4f-5718-5101-aad6-254e3d0f1a1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069628 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069628 #edb/ref 1061 170036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ed2b746-5c94-5683-9d52-e609dd44d3af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069629 #edb/ref 1061 170040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3c641ad-5eab-5dde-a062-3feb7d161f4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1061 26723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20d6dd44-7fc6-52a1-a076-27ddb6da9696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1061 4236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8854423e-52a6-5077-ab8f-c9de25d23206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1061 4237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac3a35ba-8b05-5a52-9f06-dc5add492619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1061 4242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a2dd26d-51b3-5cc1-8396-efeb5b1b8bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1061 4244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3464306e-5c3f-55f3-9c10-87ab309dded2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1061 4245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3222e5cc-52d3-5afd-8468-ab3d87c483b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1061 4295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e24a880-f9da-5e1e-b0bf-5bb2128d72a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1061 4296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eea06921-8121-51a7-86ad-15824cc00550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1061 4299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7015e37-4cd2-5ffb-814e-264ca9a0b345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1061 5059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8accb58-b4d7-5dc8-9c5e-a5ca4f3588a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1061 6170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecc30896-0712-5c97-a35e-e51485979c95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1.$ For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4,$ and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2(2x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1,$ we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a+b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+12x+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(6x)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(2x\\cdot 3)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1065 1597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d43b5e8-672e-5cdd-b7a3-8de29e6552f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1064 "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n\n$$\n-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,?\n$$\n\n \nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $−1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-20y+4 & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-20y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(10y)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(5y\\cdot 2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(25{y}^{2}-20y+4) & = \\\\ -(5y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1065 1598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "757d1827-0402-5d3d-b8da-a4dc88c254bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1065 1605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad1bc727-9054-5115-83f0-a82aa24dc35e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a-b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1065 1856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "609ce9b5-1b4e-5dfc-9d22-4ec6ea2d640b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070028 #edb/ref 1064 "What is the value of $x$ that satisfies the following equation?\n\n$$\n3^x = 3^4\n$$\n\nWe call this an **exponential equation** because the variable is contained within the exponent. \n\nNow, this equation is pretty easy to solve. Because the bases on both sides of the equation are the same (they are both equal to $3$), we can simply equate the powers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to this equation is $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070028 #edb/ref 1065 8837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070028 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fc010d0-e380-5035-bdb6-5d5d7cff060b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070028 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070029 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equation: \n\n$$\n5^x = 25\n$$\n\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nHowever, notice that $25$ can be written as a power of $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n25=5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n5^x = 5^2.\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of $5,$ we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t5^{2} &= 25 \\\n\t\t25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070029 #edb/ref 1065 8839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "43789602-55db-50d6-8517-6be1716d6133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070029 #edb/ref 1067 "Changing the Base of an Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186070092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186070093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186070094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186070095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186070096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186070094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070091 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0fb5b7f6-608c-58d4-b9fb-bb81516a054c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070092 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070092 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070092 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "832cc6f9-43ba-5a92-b599-63cd98874d91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070093 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070093 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070093 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "83d55fa4-28f6-5a90-b7c5-e9daa3ce1c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070094 #edb/ref 1080 "-6\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070094 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070094 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da7b9542-d0c9-5bb1-b3a6-5d4030d53b64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070095 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070095 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070095 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dfb95b0c-a610-5642-8da6-75c961db2d4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070096 #edb/ref 1080 "-2\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070096 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070096 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed7176ab-d3cf-50d9-8828-f90e72fadd00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Expressions Containing Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1a31c2b6-585a-5715-98f8-4b82605f4c22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "622cd109-3764-5f32-8205-046be4b57c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Square Root Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2d8a8280-5248-57e4-a5ed-14e281f3db77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1045 "Adding and Subtracting Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "17691de4-8d73-56a0-9388-fcd801f22dd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186070091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074001 #edb/ref 1071 "$4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b18991a6-bb94-5a66-967b-9daa091157a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074001 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{12} & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}-5(2\\sqrt{3}) & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (4-10)\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ -6\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1061 36487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74aa14ec-4817-573b-816c-aa9e7cef4458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1061 36491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b782d57f-6faa-597a-9408-0b6fc960adfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1061 36619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d050b8f3-0cc1-593c-8200-076f5b4f824f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1061 36620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9fded71-c2d5-5d66-9f13-5b0253efd6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1061 4327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38f3ace0-f394-598e-8dad-37fefda9d86e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1061 4328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "459089fc-d278-5487-bc0b-e4cd6237c142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5,$ respectively. \n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}-1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ (2-1)\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1065 1609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9aa49408-30d5-57b5-8dff-ec1744f9fc6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers. \n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1065 5995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4af2af2e-0f81-5094-adf6-2ede72a493d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{2}-6\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ (4-6)\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1065 5996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32078f96-6eaf-57d2-bc71-8af8495d5d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Expressions With Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186075906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186075904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075901 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4215e270-50a3-50bb-b7ed-bf7f3eb3d7b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075902 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075902 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d49c7bc-c10c-5365-bdcf-53015828e98b" #edb/ref 175